<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Visvaldis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 1200–1239</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Vissewalde, Vsevolod of Jersika</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Visvaldis, called Vissewalde in German sources, was the prince of Jersika on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-dvina">Daugava</a> in present-day Latvia in the early 13th century. Of mixed Latgalian and Rus' background and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a>, he resisted the Livonian crusaders, but after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-of-riga">Albert of Riga</a> sacked Jersika in 1209 he had to accept part of his lands back as a fief from the bishop. He is a figure of Latvian national memory.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/visvaldis.jpg" alt="Visvaldis" width="320" height="456" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Girish Mahajan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Principality-of-jersika-12b2d0c7-a69e-4775-8648-6778f70eddd-resize-750.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
